(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-weight-control-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Weight Control (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"How does lecture characterize obesity as a medical condition?","choices":["A condition that always resolves completely on its own without any medical intervention.","A condition entirely unrelated to any identifiable or treatable medical process.","A chronic disease requiring sustainable lifestyle changes, not short-term crash diets.","A temporary lifestyle inconvenience carrying no long-term medical significance."],"answer":2,"exp":"Obesity is described as a chronic disease requiring sustainable lifestyle changes — not the short-term, rapid weight loss seen in crash diets, which is the primary reason so many people regain lost weight. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is the recommended rate of weight loss in a sound weight-loss plan, per lecture?","choices":["0.5 to 1 pound every 2 weeks","As fast as possible, regardless of rate","1 to 2 pounds per week","5 pounds per week"],"answer":2,"exp":"A sound weight-loss plan encourages slow and steady loss of 1 to 2 pounds per week, rather than rapid weight loss. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Per lecture, after 10% of body weight is lost in a weight-loss plan, what should follow?","choices":["Switch immediately to a very-low-calorie diet to accelerate further weight loss.","A period of weight maintenance for a few months, then evaluate the need for further weight loss.","Stop tracking food intake entirely, since the goal weight has been reached.","Immediately begin an even more aggressive phase of calorie restriction and exercise."],"answer":1,"exp":"A sound weight-loss plan includes a period of weight maintenance for a few months after 10% of body weight is lost, and then evaluates whether further weight loss is needed. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are the three key principles of the weight-loss triad, per lecture?","choices":["Restricting calories, adding physical activity, and adding an appropriate behavioral component","Intermittent fasting, whole-body detoxing, and multi-day juice cleanses","Bariatric surgery, prescription medication, and dietary supplements","Genetic testing, prepackaged meal kits, and one-on-one personal training"],"answer":0,"exp":"The weight-loss triad consists of restricting calories, adding physical activity, and adding an appropriate behavioral component, ideally with guidance from a registered dietitian nutritionist. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the average daily calorie need range given for women, per lecture?","choices":["1,800 to 2,400 kcal","1,200 to 1,600 kcal","3,500 to 4,000 kcal","2,400 to 3,200 kcal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Average daily calorie need for women is 1,800 to 2,400 kcal per day, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the average daily calorie need range given for men, per lecture?","choices":["500 to 800 kcal","2,400 to 3,200 kcal","1,200 to 1,600 kcal","1,800 to 2,400 kcal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Average daily calorie need for men is 2,400 to 3,200 kcal per day, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"About how much should daily calorie intake be reduced to achieve roughly 1 pound of fat loss per week, per lecture?","choices":["3,500 kcal per day","100 kcal per day","500 kcal per day","1,500 kcal per day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Limiting calorie intake by about 500 kcal per day is the goal for losing about 1 pound of stored fat per week. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which dietary pattern is described as most successful in long-term weight-loss studies, per lecture?","choices":["Very-low-calorie liquid-only pattern","Primarily plant-based, high-fiber dietary pattern","High-fat, low-carbohydrate pattern","A pattern with no dietary pattern at all, focused only on exercise"],"answer":1,"exp":"A primarily plant-based, high-fiber dietary pattern is described as most successful in long-term weight-loss studies. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Per lecture, how long does it typically take nerves in the stomach to signal the brain that a person is full?","choices":["Almost instantly","About 24 hours","About 2 hours","Up to 20 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nerves in the stomach can take up to 20 minutes to signal the brain that a person is full, which is why eating slowly helps control hunger. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which strategy is listed to help increase satiety and control hunger, per lecture?","choices":["Eating quickly to finish before hunger signals arrive","Skipping meals entirely to reduce daily total caloric intake","Deliberately avoiding all water intake between scheduled meals","Eating high-fiber, high-volume foods rich in water, and eating slowly"],"answer":3,"exp":"High-fiber snacks, high-volume foods rich in water and fiber, drinking water between meals, lean proteins, and eating slowly are all listed strategies to increase satiety. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What typically happens to the composition of weight lost during the early phase of a weight-loss plan, per lecture?","choices":["Early weight loss is larger and includes fluid and fat.","No measurable weight loss occurs during the early phase of a new diet.","Weight loss is perfectly linear throughout a diet.","Early weight loss consists purely of muscle mass."],"answer":0,"exp":"Early weight loss is larger and includes both fluid and fat; the rate begins to slow as muscle mass increases with continued exercise. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What are the \"3 Ms\" of weight-loss maintenance and relapse prevention, per lecture?","choices":["Meal-planning, Mindfulness, Maintenance","Medication, Meals, Metabolism","Measurement, Moderation, Motivation","Motivation, Movement, and Monitoring"],"answer":3,"exp":"The \"3 Ms\" of weight-loss maintenance are Motivation, Movement, and Monitoring. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is recommended when a person has a minor setback (a \"lapse\") in a weight-loss plan, per lecture?","choices":["Immediately restrict calories far below the original plan as a form of self-punishment for the lapse.","Wait several full weeks before even attempting to resume any of the original healthy habits.","Do not overreact, take charge immediately, and modify internal language so the lapse doesn't collapse all effort.","Give up on the entire plan permanently, since the setback proves it was never going to work."],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture recommends not overreacting, taking charge immediately, and modifying internal language (e.g. reframing a slip positively) so that one lapse doesn't collapse all prior effort. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is mindful eating, per lecture?","choices":["Eating as quickly as possible without any attention to hunger cues or fullness signals, purely to save time between tasks.","Avoiding all social eating occasions such as restaurants, parties, and family meals in order to control intake.","Eating exclusively pre-packaged, portion-controlled meals purchased from a specialty weight-loss company.","Being consciously aware of the entire eating experience, from preparation to consumption, including hunger cues, satiety, flavor, and texture."],"answer":3,"exp":"Mindful eating means being consciously aware of the entire eating experience — from food preparation to consumption — recognizing hunger cues, satiety, flavor, taste, and texture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does \"chain-breaking\" refer to as a behavioral weight-loss strategy, per lecture?","choices":["A specific type of high-intensity resistance-training exercise routine performed several times weekly.","Separating behaviors that tend to occur together, such as overindulging on chips while watching television.","Deliberately breaking an extended fast after a lengthy period of intentional caloric restriction.","Breaking down one large meal into several smaller courses served throughout the evening."],"answer":1,"exp":"Chain-breaking separates behaviors that tend to occur together, such as overindulging on potato chips while watching television, to disrupt the automatic pairing. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What does \"stimulus control\" refer to as a behavioral weight-loss strategy, per lecture?","choices":["A surgical technique used to physically reduce overall stomach volume.","Taking prescription medication that directly suppresses the appetite stimulus.","Putting a person in charge of managing temptations in their environment.","A method of automatically counting and logging calories through a smartphone app."],"answer":2,"exp":"Stimulus control puts a person in charge of their temptations, such as storing food out of sight or avoiding buffet-style meals. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which shopping-related behavioral tactic is recommended for weight loss, per lecture?","choices":["Shop for groceries while hungry, to better judge realistic portion sizes.","Shop for food after eating, so you are not hungry, and shop from a list.","Avoid the produce section entirely in order to save time while shopping.","Buy only highly processed, ready-to-eat convenience foods."],"answer":1,"exp":"Shopping for food after eating (not while hungry) and shopping from a list or app are recommended tactics, along with shopping fresh foods around the store's perimeter. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which eating-behavior tactic is recommended during meals, per lecture?","choices":["Put the fork down between bites and chew thoroughly and slowly.","Always finish every item on the plate.","Eat as quickly as possible to reduce time spent thinking about food.","Eat while distracted by texting or watching television to reduce boredom."],"answer":0,"exp":"Putting the fork down between bites, chewing thoroughly and slowly, leaving some food on the plate, and avoiding distraction while eating are recommended eating-behavior tactics. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What portion-control tactic is suggested for restaurant dining, per lecture?","choices":["Order the entrée and share it, order a cup of soup instead of a bowl, or use a take-home box for half the entrée.","Always order the largest available portion size on the menu, since it offers the best overall value.","Avoid eating at restaurants entirely under all circumstances, even for special occasions.","Skip the appetizer and entrée entirely and order only the largest dessert on the menu."],"answer":0,"exp":"Suggested portion-control tactics include sharing an entrée, choosing a cup instead of a bowl of soup, ordering an appetizer instead of an entrée, or packing half the entrée in a take-home box. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What BMI criteria make a patient a general candidate for weight-loss medication, per lecture?","choices":["BMI ≥18.5 alone, with no comorbid conditions required at any level","BMI ≥30, or BMI ≥27 with at least one obesity-associated comorbid condition","BMI ≥25 alone, regardless of whether any comorbid conditions are present","BMI ≥40 alone, with no lower threshold option available for comorbid patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Candidates for weight-loss medication generally have a BMI ≥30, or a BMI ≥27 with at least one obesity-associated comorbid condition. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How does orlistat work as a weight-loss medication, per lecture?","choices":["It slows gastric emptying by roughly 30% and directly enhances feelings of satiety.","It blocks approximately 30% of carbohydrate absorption occurring in the colon.","It suppresses appetite by acting directly on hunger centers in the brain.","It reduces fat digestion by about 30% by inhibiting the lipase enzyme in the small intestine."],"answer":3,"exp":"Orlistat reduces fat digestion by about 30% by inhibiting lipase enzyme action in the small intestine. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which weight-loss medication is described as slowing gastric emptying and enhancing satiety, per lecture?","choices":["Liraglutide (Saxenda®)","Contrave (bupropion/naltrexone)","Phentermine (Adipex-P®)","Orlistat (Xenical®)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Liraglutide (Saxenda®) slows gastric emptying and enhances satiety. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What BMI criteria qualify a patient for bariatric surgery, per lecture?","choices":["BMI ≥50 alone, with no lower comorbidity-based threshold","BMI ≥40, or BMI ≥35 with obesity-related comorbid conditions","BMI ≥25 alone, regardless of comorbid conditions","BMI ≥30 alone, without any comorbidity requirement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bariatric surgery candidates generally have a BMI ≥40, or a BMI ≥35 with obesity-related comorbid conditions. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is a very-low-calorie diet (VLCD), also known as a protein-sparing modified fast, per lecture?","choices":["A diet providing 400 to 800 kcal per day, often in liquid form, administered under strict medical supervision.","A liquid diet that eliminates protein entirely while allowing unrestricted carbohydrate and fat intake.","A diet providing 1,800 to 2,400 kcal per day without any medical supervision required.","An entirely unsupervised home diet with no minimum calorie floor and no medical monitoring."],"answer":0,"exp":"A VLCD (protein-sparing modified fast) provides 400 to 800 kcal per day, often in liquid form, and is administered under strict medical supervision. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which bariatric procedure creates a small stomach pouch and reroutes food to bypass part of the small intestine, per lecture?","choices":["Vertical sleeve gastrectomy","AspireAssist®","Gastric bypass","Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gastric bypass creates a small pouch that limits stomach capacity, and food bypasses a portion of the small intestine, reducing calorie and nutrient absorption. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which bariatric procedure has the lowest risk of vitamin and mineral deficits and can be reversed, per lecture?","choices":["Ileal transposition, a lengthier and more complex operation","Vertical sleeve gastrectomy, an irreversible restrictive procedure","Gastric bypass, a malabsorptive and difficult-to-reverse procedure","Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding has the lowest risk of vitamin and mineral deficits among the described procedures, and can be reversed. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What BMI defines underweight, per lecture?","choices":["Below 16.0","Below 22.0","Below 18.5","Below 20.0"],"answer":2,"exp":"Underweight is defined as a BMI below 18.5. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"About how many people lived in food-insecure households in the US in 2022, per lecture?","choices":["44.2 million","4.4 million","144 million","14.2 million"],"answer":0,"exp":"In 2022, 44.2 million people lived in food-insecure households in the US, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a resource to help combat food insecurity, per lecture?","choices":["OSHA workplace safety grant funding programs","Medicare Part D prescription drug coverage","WIC (Women, Infants, and Children program)","FEMA emergency disaster relief loan programs"],"answer":2,"exp":"WIC is listed among the resources to combat undernutrition/food insecurity, along with Summer EBT, school lunch/breakfast programs, the Child and Adult Care Food Program, and afterschool nutrition programs. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which warning sign is listed as characteristic of an unreliable (\"fad\") diet, per lecture?","choices":["Encourages gradual weight loss over many months.","Encourages regular physical activity alongside dietary changes.","Recommends a registered dietitian nutritionist for guidance.","Promotes rapid weight loss and uses personal testimonials as evidence."],"answer":3,"exp":"Fad diets typically promote rapid weight loss, limit or restrict food selections, use personal testimonials, claim to be a cure-all for everyone, and recommend expensive supplements. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 80"}]};
